  "body": {
    "id": "thm:two-cell-frontier",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "When \\(m=2\\), \\(\\mathcal R(n,2,\\tau)\\asymp_{\\beta,D}\\ell n^{-\\varkappa}\\) uniformly for \\(n\\ge2\\) and \\(0<\\tau\\le1/(8n)\\). Consequently, along every two-cell sequence, \\(\\mathcal R(n,2,\\tau_n)\\to0\\) if and only if \\(\\log(1/\\tau_n)=o(n^{\\varkappa})\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(m\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal R(n,m,\\tau)\\)",
      "\\(n\\)",
      "\\(\\tau\\)",
      "\\(\\ell\\)",
      "\\(\\varkappa\\)",
      "\\(\\beta\\)",
      "\\(D\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:generic-regret-sandwich"
    ],
    "justification": "The exactly matched two-cell case is a falsifiable boundary condition for every proposed many-cell frontier.",
    "gap": "GuggenbergerMehtaPavlov2026 finds finite-sample fixed-quantile minimax degeneracy for bounded outcomes; Hall extrapolation produces a nondegenerate moving-tail rate even with only two cells.",
    "consumer": "It tells an experiment with one capacity-constrained pair exactly how fast the deployment quantile may move beyond the sample while policy learning remains possible.",
    "proof_tex": "Set \\(m=2\\) in \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}generic\\text{-}regret\\text{-}sandwich}\\).  Since \\(2\\), \\(\\log4\\), and their positive powers are constants, its lower and upper bounds become\n\\[\nc_{\\beta,D}\\ell n^{-\\varkappa}\n\\leq\\mathcal R(n,2,\\tau)\n\\leq C_{\\beta,D}\\ell n^{-\\varkappa},\n\\]\nafter changing constants to cover the finitely bounded factors \\(\\min\\{1,(2/n)^\\varkappa\\}\\) and \\(\\min\\{1,(2\\log4/n)^\\varkappa\\}\\) for \\(n\\geq2\\).  This is the asserted uniform equivalence.  Along a two-cell sequence with \\(n\\to\\infty\\), the two positive constant bounds imply\n\\[\n\\mathcal R(n,2,\\tau_n)\\to0\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\ell_n n^{-\\varkappa}\\to0\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\log(1/\\tau_n)=o(n^\\varkappa).\n\\]",
